WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.120 --> 00:00:02.190
We're now at the part of our conversation

00:00:02.190 --> 00:00:04.530
where I can answer the
question we started off with,

00:00:04.530 --> 00:00:06.423
which is, why do you breathe?

00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:08.730
And we talked about how,

00:00:08.730 --> 00:00:10.320
eh, it's oxygen and we need that.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.490
Well, oxygen is not really
a fuel for metabolism.

00:00:14.490 --> 00:00:17.460
It's needed to go through
aerobic metabolism,

00:00:17.460 --> 00:00:21.480
but the fuel is coming from
your fat and carbohydrates.

00:00:21.480 --> 00:00:24.120
I only need oxygen during
aerobic metabolism,

00:00:24.120 --> 00:00:25.860
but I'm very effective at anaerobic,

00:00:25.860 --> 00:00:29.820
which means I can produce energy
without the need of oxygen.

00:00:29.820 --> 00:00:32.430
But to finish those processes,

00:00:32.430 --> 00:00:34.500
I've got to have the oxygen around.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:36.840
And so it's a little bit of a twist here.

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:38.100
This is also explaining

00:00:38.100 --> 00:00:40.560
why even if you're an anaerobic athlete,

00:00:40.560 --> 00:00:43.680
you still care deeply
about your aerobic system

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:46.410
because this is what
allows you to recover,

00:00:46.410 --> 00:00:49.380
to completely metabolize
your carbohydrates,

00:00:49.380 --> 00:00:52.020
to finish that process
and restore yourself

00:00:52.020 --> 00:00:53.370
back to homeostasis.

00:00:53.370 --> 00:00:54.870
The faster you can do that,

00:00:54.870 --> 00:00:57.870
the faster you can repeat
your anaerobic processes.

00:00:57.870 --> 00:01:00.210
You can recover, you can
get back to training,

00:01:00.210 --> 00:01:01.680
get back to competition.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:04.260
The more you practice, the better you get,

00:01:04.260 --> 00:01:06.240
the better you perform.

00:01:06.240 --> 00:01:08.550
And so what's actually
fully happening is this.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.100
When you take a breath in and you inhale,

00:01:11.100 --> 00:01:13.200
you're bringing in amongst other things,

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:15.390
but primarily oxygen.

00:01:15.390 --> 00:01:18.510
When you take a breath out,
you're breathing out CO2.

00:01:18.510 --> 00:01:22.470
The oxygen you bring in is primarily there

00:01:22.470 --> 00:01:24.570
to regulate metabolic processes.

00:01:24.570 --> 00:01:28.800
But the CO2 you're exhaling
is regulating your pH.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:29.790
Now there's a handful of things

00:01:29.790 --> 00:01:32.490
your body will regulate
almost anything else.

00:01:32.490 --> 00:01:35.010
One of them is your pH.

00:01:35.010 --> 00:01:37.080
It does not like to mess with this.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:39.150
If you were to look at other markers

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:40.140
like say your blood glucose,

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:42.030
you realize that that's highly variable.

00:01:42.030 --> 00:01:45.840
It can be as low as, you know,
70 milligrams per deciliter,

00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:48.780
as high as 150 during exercise
or something like that.

00:01:48.780 --> 00:01:50.400
And so you can see it all double

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:53.760
or maybe even triple
the amount in the blood.

00:01:53.760 --> 00:01:55.620
You would never do that with pH.

00:01:55.620 --> 00:01:57.420
It has an extremely tight window

00:01:57.420 --> 00:01:59.100
that it will not move out of.

00:01:59.100 --> 00:02:01.860
And that's because all the
enzymes that are required

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:04.530
for you to go through
any metabolic process

00:02:04.530 --> 00:02:06.360
need to be in a certain pH range.

00:02:06.360 --> 00:02:07.230
If it gets out of that,

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:10.080
it becomes too acidic or too alkalotic.

00:02:10.080 --> 00:02:12.600
They can't function,
you can't create energy.

00:02:12.600 --> 00:02:13.980
You're going to die very, very quickly.

00:02:13.980 --> 00:02:18.980
So pH is insanely important
to hold into a tight window.

00:02:20.100 --> 00:02:22.110
And so what happens is

00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:26.220
you don't feel that air hunger
or that desire to breathe

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.170
because oxygen starts getting low.

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:30.720
Remember, especially at rest
or even during exercise,

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:34.230
you can produce energy anaerobically.

00:02:34.230 --> 00:02:37.350
So when you start getting low on oxygen,

00:02:37.350 --> 00:02:39.060
you'll just switch to
anaerobic metabolism.

00:02:39.060 --> 00:02:42.270
It's not necessarily a reason
for you to panic, to stress,

00:02:42.270 --> 00:02:43.620
or to change your behavior.

00:02:44.670 --> 00:02:48.750
Increases in CO2 though will do that.

00:02:48.750 --> 00:02:50.940
And so remember your muscle is,

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:54.600
whether it's using fat as a
fuel or carbohydrates as a fuel,

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:57.030
it's trying to generate
a molecule called ATP.

00:02:57.030 --> 00:02:59.820
This is the energy
currency in all of biology.

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:02.490
And it doesn't matter what you
use that ATP for by the way.

00:03:02.490 --> 00:03:04.320
It doesn't matter if we're
talking about skeletal muscle,

00:03:04.320 --> 00:03:06.750
we're talking about cardiac
muscle or anything else.

00:03:06.750 --> 00:03:08.670
So whether you're using this for exercise

00:03:08.670 --> 00:03:10.950
to power your brain to
recover, to digest food,

00:03:10.950 --> 00:03:12.150
it's irrelevant here, right?

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:14.640
We're going to use
carbohydrates or fat as a fuel,

00:03:14.640 --> 00:03:16.350
we're going to make ATP.

00:03:16.350 --> 00:03:17.220
And then at the end,

00:03:17.220 --> 00:03:21.450
the final byproduct of all
metabolism is going to be

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:24.810
water, CO2, and ATP.

00:03:24.810 --> 00:03:27.330
So the CO2 concentration increases

00:03:27.330 --> 00:03:29.763
as metabolic rate increases.

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:31.710
As a result of that,

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:36.710
you start then moving CO2 from
your tissue into the blood.

00:03:37.140 --> 00:03:40.860
Concentrations of CO2
then and blood go up.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:42.600
You've got chemoreceptors

00:03:42.600 --> 00:03:45.060
in your brainstem and various other places

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:46.800
that are going to be
paying extreme attention

00:03:46.800 --> 00:03:48.540
to the amount of CO2 in your blood.

00:03:48.540 --> 00:03:50.670
If CO2 gets really, really high,

00:03:50.670 --> 00:03:52.920
we call this hypercapnia.

00:03:52.920 --> 00:03:55.113
If it gets low, it's called hypocapnia.

00:03:56.070 --> 00:03:57.930
Remember those terms.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:01.290
So hypercapnia increases
in CO2 concentration

00:04:01.290 --> 00:04:05.010
actually signal your red blood cells

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.470
to drop the oxygen on them,

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:09.870
making it easier for your muscle

00:04:09.870 --> 00:04:12.120
to extract and absorb the oxygen.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:14.160
Effectively you think about it this way.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:16.410
If CO2 is high in the blood,

00:04:16.410 --> 00:04:17.970
your body is under the assumption

00:04:17.970 --> 00:04:19.860
you're going through a lot of metabolism,

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:22.170
so it's under the assumption
that we want to use

00:04:22.170 --> 00:04:23.280
and need a lot of oxygen.

00:04:23.280 --> 00:04:25.140
So it reduces that affinity.

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.450
And this is called the Bohr effect.

00:04:27.450 --> 00:04:30.960
If you get hypocapnic,
again too low of CO2,

00:04:30.960 --> 00:04:32.940
it does the opposite.

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:35.340
Now this is going to be counterintuitive

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:37.500
when we talk about
things like CO2 tolerance

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:39.420
and respiratory rate in future episodes

00:04:39.420 --> 00:04:42.030
as to why you could
potentially have problems

00:04:42.030 --> 00:04:44.340
with hyperventilation or over-breathing.

00:04:44.340 --> 00:04:45.510
So what's happening in this context is

00:04:45.510 --> 00:04:47.400
those signals are being sent to your brain

00:04:47.400 --> 00:04:49.080
and that is interpreting it as saying

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:52.740
CO2 is too high, let's reduce that.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.040
The way you reduce CO2
concentrations in your blood

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.153
is to exhale.

00:04:57.990 --> 00:04:59.040
And so this would cause you

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.920
to increase your respiratory rate

00:05:01.920 --> 00:05:04.920
and to start either mildly or
excessively hyperventilating.

00:05:04.920 --> 00:05:08.400
And this is why as you
exercise your respiratory rate,

00:05:08.400 --> 00:05:12.000
again the amount of breaths
you're taking, goes up.

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:16.530
It is in part to increase and
bring in oxygen, of course.

00:05:16.530 --> 00:05:17.790
But when we're doing it anaerobically,

00:05:17.790 --> 00:05:19.050
we're not using oxygen anyways.

00:05:19.050 --> 00:05:21.150
So the real reason we're breathing so hard

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:22.225
and we're panting,

00:05:22.225 --> 00:05:23.070
[Andy breathing deeply]

00:05:23.070 --> 00:05:23.903
and all that stuff

00:05:23.903 --> 00:05:26.310
as we're getting harder and
harder of our exercise is

00:05:26.310 --> 00:05:27.630
because we're trying to dump

00:05:27.630 --> 00:05:30.420
and get rid of all that CO2 buildup.

00:05:30.420 --> 00:05:35.420
Remember, excess CO2 is altering pH.

00:05:35.520 --> 00:05:37.080
This is making us more acidic.

00:05:37.080 --> 00:05:38.880
This becomes an extreme problem.

00:05:38.880 --> 00:05:40.620
And so another way to think about this is,

00:05:40.620 --> 00:05:45.620
when you inhale, that's
actually a sympathetic driver.

00:05:46.290 --> 00:05:49.290
And so your heart rate
increases during inhalation.

00:05:49.290 --> 00:05:54.290
When you exhale, it is
parasympathetic and it drops.

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:57.000
So effectively what's
happening is your body is

00:05:57.000 --> 00:05:59.220
sort of saying, oh, you're inhaling,

00:05:59.220 --> 00:06:01.080
we're assuming then
you're bringing in oxygen.

00:06:01.080 --> 00:06:03.270
Let's get prepared to deliver this oxygen

00:06:03.270 --> 00:06:04.350
throughout the system.

00:06:04.350 --> 00:06:07.170
When you're exhaling, it's the opposite.

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:08.700
I don't want to be in a situation

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:09.990
where I'm hyperventilating.

00:06:09.990 --> 00:06:13.500
I don't need to be breathing
too much because if again,

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:15.750
that CO2 gets too low,

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:17.340
instead of being acidic,

00:06:17.340 --> 00:06:19.800
we are now in respiratory alkalosis.

00:06:19.800 --> 00:06:21.270
So the opposite direction, right?

00:06:21.270 --> 00:06:22.650
We're too basic.

00:06:22.650 --> 00:06:24.330
And so it slows the heart rate down.

00:06:24.330 --> 00:06:26.700
So every time you take a breath in,

00:06:26.700 --> 00:06:28.230
your heart rate jumps up a little bit.

00:06:28.230 --> 00:06:31.350
Every time you take a breath
out, it goes down a little bit.

00:06:31.350 --> 00:06:34.140
So if I'm altering my respiratory rate,

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:35.850
I'm then altering my heart rate.

00:06:35.850 --> 00:06:40.530
And this is why things like
HRV are so intrinsically tied

00:06:40.530 --> 00:06:42.300
to things like respiratory rate.

00:06:42.300 --> 00:06:43.500
I can't let us move off this point

00:06:43.500 --> 00:06:45.000
without saying one final thing.

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.850
I know we want to get to our
three Is here in one second.

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:49.620
But a lot of people are aware,

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:51.720
and in the coaching world,

00:06:51.720 --> 00:06:54.240
people use HRV very often,

00:06:54.240 --> 00:06:56.430
and there's a lot of data to support this.

00:06:56.430 --> 00:06:58.200
There's a lot of critical
information we can get

00:06:58.200 --> 00:06:59.760
for assessing, say,

00:06:59.760 --> 00:07:03.690
exercise volume, fatigue,
readiness, and things like that.

00:07:03.690 --> 00:07:05.040
Tons of value there.

00:07:05.040 --> 00:07:06.780
But I don't think enough
people are paying attention

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:07.920
to respiratory rate.

00:07:07.920 --> 00:07:09.240
This is really highlighted

00:07:09.240 --> 00:07:11.880
in a paper that just came
out in the last few months,

00:07:11.880 --> 00:07:13.770
and so I'd like to bring
this to your attention.

00:07:13.770 --> 00:07:16.980
What they did is looked
at college-aged students

00:07:16.980 --> 00:07:19.440
and they simply measured
their respiratory rate.

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:21.840
And one of the things that they
found that's interesting is

00:07:21.840 --> 00:07:25.560
for every breath per minute that increase,

00:07:25.560 --> 00:07:28.260
so if a respiratory rate went
from 15 breaths per minute

00:07:28.260 --> 00:07:29.940
to 16 breaths per minute,

00:07:29.940 --> 00:07:32.610
they increased their likelihood
of experiencing stress

00:07:32.610 --> 00:07:34.143
by 1.25X.

00:07:34.980 --> 00:07:37.470
And what I found particularly
interesting about this is

00:07:37.470 --> 00:07:42.337
they found that irrespective
of changes in things like HRV,

00:07:43.290 --> 00:07:46.440
total hours of sleep, sleep
efficiency, sleep onset,

00:07:46.440 --> 00:07:48.780
and various other things
that are typically

00:07:48.780 --> 00:07:52.020
the metrics used to measure overall stress

00:07:52.020 --> 00:07:53.580
and autonomic nervous system functionality

00:07:53.580 --> 00:07:54.960
and things like that.

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.090
And so what we're going
over here is not to say

00:07:57.090 --> 00:07:59.310
that HRV or sleep are
not good metrics to take,

00:07:59.310 --> 00:08:00.450
they clearly are.

00:08:00.450 --> 00:08:02.370
It's just that you're going to find things

00:08:02.370 --> 00:08:03.540
in the respiratory rate

00:08:03.540 --> 00:08:05.460
that you're not necessarily
going to see in other places

00:08:05.460 --> 00:08:08.940
that give you great clues
about overall stress.

00:08:08.940 --> 00:08:09.960
So strongly encourage you

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:11.940
to pay attention to respiratory rate.

00:08:11.940 --> 00:08:14.290
And we'll talk about that
plenty in the future.

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:16.761
[music]

